# Workflow Rule

- Source of truth order: `{{ manifesto_path }}` -> `ai.config.yaml` -> `openspec/config.yaml` and the selected OpenSpec change -> `.ai/rules/*` -> `.ai/skills/*` -> `.ai/reference/*` -> `Taskfile.yml`
- Generic execution gates live in `.ai/rules/workflow-gates.mdc`.
- Shared rule and skill sources are projected into provider runtimes via symlinks; update `.ai/*` first.
- Stack-specific and repo-specific gates must layer on top through profile-specific rules instead of mutating the generic gate file.
- `.ai/rules/project-profile.mdc` is the canonical repo-local rule layer generated from `ai.config.yaml`; always read it before executing any skill.
- If `.ai/rules/project-profile.mdc` contains `## Project-Specific Rules`, treat them as mandatory repository overrides on top of the generic workflow rules.
- `/implement` must track per-rule compliance in `progress.md` (`## Project Rules Compliance`); `/review` must verify each rule with explicit pass/fail evidence before a clean verdict.
- Use canonical tasks only:
  - `{{ task_command }} {{ task_implement_name }}`
  - `{{ task_command }} {{ task_test_name }}`
  - `{{ task_command }} {{ task_test_scoped_name }} -- <targets...>`
  - `{{ task_command }} {{ task_lint_name }}`
  - `{{ task_command }} {{ task_lint_scoped_name }} -- <targets...>`
  - `{{ task_command }} {{ task_verify_name }}`
  - `{{ task_command }} {{ task_review_name }}`
- Do not hardcode stack-specific commands in generic workflow prompts or status updates.
- For non-trivial work, planning and execution memory must live in explicit local artifacts:
  - `openspec/changes/<change-id>/` for proposal, delta specs, design, and tasks
  - `{{ plan_progress_runtime_root }}/ISSUE-ID/progress.md` (`PROGRESS::ISSUE-ID` marker block inside the file) for execution evidence
- When a tracker is enabled, use it for issue workflow **states** only; do not mirror plan/progress bodies into tracker comments.
- OpenSpec change quality minimum:
  - proposal intent, scope, non-goals, and impact
  - testable delta requirements and scenarios
  - design decisions, invariants, migration, rollback, and `Claimed Write Scope`
  - tasks mapped to requirements and canonical verification commands
  - no unresolved implementation blockers
{% if minimalism_enabled and minimalism_level != "off" -%}
  - `Minimal Solution Ladder`
{% endif -%}
- Progress artifact minimum:
  - `Выполнено`
  - `Текущий шаг`
  - `Дальше`
  - `Spec Conformance`
  - `Project Rules Compliance`
{% if minimalism_enabled and minimalism_level != "off" -%}
  - `Minimal Solution Conformance`
{% endif -%}
  - `Решения по ходу`
- If work is orchestrator-managed, reservation/worktree ownership must live in `.ai/runtime/orchestrator-state.mjs` and `.ai/runtime/orchestrator/runs/ISSUE-ID.json`.
- Multi-issue work uses the host product's Goal mode as its only long-running manager:
  - the main Goal thread selects runnable work, delegates it, verifies evidence, integrates accepted output, and does not edit product code;
  - one native worker owns exactly one issue, one OpenSpec `changeId`, and one repository-local orchestrator worktree;
  - the worker runs `/orchestrator`, and its implementation phase uses `openspec-apply-change <changeId>` plus `openspec instructions apply --change <changeId>`;
  - native host subagent tools are mandatory; recursive agent CLI launches, PTY polling, and a project-owned manager daemon are not fallback mechanisms;
  - worker context uses no inherited turns or the smallest bounded history plus exact artifact paths; full-history forks are not the default;
  - wait once on native completion/message events; repeated short status/list polling is forbidden;
  - unavailable model preferences fall back only to a compatible native or inherited model, with the fallback recorded;
  - dependent work waits for accepted review, full verification, terminal OpenSpec sync/archive, and integration into the next base;
  - Goal state is rehydrated from the initiative manifest, OpenSpec, progress, and orchestrator artifacts; do not create a duplicate ledger;
  - repository, conflict, approval, live MR, deployment, failed full-suite, and human-decision boundaries pause execution.
- Workflow language contract is `{{ workflow_language }}` for reasoning, progress artifacts, review artifacts, and user-facing workflow dialogue unless the user explicitly requests another language.
- Completion means all required OpenSpec, implementation, verification, and review points are done; partial completion is not completion.
{% if minimalism_enabled and minimalism_level != "off" -%}
- When `minimalism.enabled` is true, read `.ai/rules/minimal-solution.mdc` before `/plan`, `/implement`, and `/review`. The orchestrator does not run a separate pre-review simplify gate.
{% endif -%}
- The default post-implementation order is `implement -> scoped handoff test + lint -> review -> full verify`.
- The handoff evidence is bound to the current workspace fingerprint and is reused after a clean read-only review.
- If the initial review produces `high`, `critical`, or `medium` findings, fix them and rerun affected scoped test + lint. Run at most one delta-only closure review when risk requires it; a second blocking verdict is a human gate.
- `review` is semantic/findings-only and never invokes full `verify`. Full verify runs once per fingerprint under the generated host-wide lease, except one exact flaky rerun followed by at most one final full retry.
